1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:04,979
In the middle of the Texas prairie, about
thirty miles south of Dallas,

2
00:00:05,299 --> 00:00:09,396
there's a cluster of empty buildings
surrounded by cattle fields.

3
00:00:09,716 --> 00:00:15,249
No signs. No museum. From the highway, it
looks like a warehouse park nobody

4
00:00:15,249 --> 00:00:15,904
finished.

5
00:00:16,224 --> 00:00:21,406
Underneath those fields is a tunnel. A
concrete tube bored through solid chalk,

6
00:00:21,726 --> 00:00:23,456
more than fourteen miles long.

7
00:00:23,776 --> 00:00:28,675
It was supposed to be the first piece of
the biggest scientific machine ever

8
00:00:28,675 --> 00:00:29,320
attempted.

9
00:00:29,640 --> 00:00:30,827
But it was abandoned.

10
00:00:31,147 --> 00:00:36,443
For six years, thousands of engineers and
physicists worked toward a single goal:

11
00:00:36,763 --> 00:00:39,059
build a ring fifty-four miles around,

12
00:00:39,379 --> 00:00:42,497
smash particles together harder than
anyone ever had,

13
00:00:42,817 --> 00:00:45,658
and find out what the universe is actually
made of.

14
00:00:45,978 --> 00:00:52,064
By 1993, they'd dug more than fourteen
miles of tunnel and spent two billion

15
00:00:52,064 --> 00:00:52,705
dollars.

16
00:00:53,025 --> 00:00:55,716
They needed fifty-four. They never got
there.

17
00:00:56,036 --> 00:00:59,664
This is the story of the Superconducting
Super Collider.

18
00:00:59,984 --> 00:01:02,956
Why did America try to build it? What went
wrong?

19
00:01:03,276 --> 00:01:07,966
And why did the United States walk away
from the biggest machine in history?

20
00:01:12,086 --> 00:01:15,081
By the nineteen-eighties, physicists had a
problem.

21
00:01:15,401 --> 00:01:20,326
Their best theory of matter, the Standard
Model, worked almost perfectly. But it had

22
00:01:20,326 --> 00:01:21,910
a hole in the middle of it.

23
00:01:22,230 --> 00:01:27,825
The theory needed one more particle, the
Higgs boson, to explain why anything has

24
00:01:27,825 --> 00:01:28,654
mass at all.

25
00:01:28,974 --> 00:01:34,545
Nobody had ever seen it. And the only way
to find it was to recreate the conditions

26
00:01:34,545 --> 00:01:37,364
a fraction of a second after the Big Bang.

27
00:01:37,684 --> 00:01:43,050
That meant a particle accelerator. A ring
of magnets that pushes protons to nearly

28
00:01:43,050 --> 00:01:46,453
the speed of light, then slams them into
each other.

29
00:01:46,773 --> 00:01:51,227
The bigger the ring, the harder the
collision. The harder the collision, the

30
00:01:51,227 --> 00:01:53,162
more of the universe you can see.

31
00:01:53,482 --> 00:01:57,559
Europe was already building big machines
at CERN, near Geneva.

32
00:01:57,879 --> 00:02:03,136
American physics had led the world since
the Second World War. Losing that lead was

33
00:02:03,136 --> 00:02:04,023
not an option.

34
00:02:04,343 --> 00:02:10,718
So in 1987, Ronald Reagan approved the
most ambitious science project the country

35
00:02:10,718 --> 00:02:12,843
had attempted since Apollo.

36
00:02:13,163 --> 00:02:16,482
His advice to the team was two words.
Throw deep.

37
00:02:16,802 --> 00:02:21,183
The design was enormous. A ring 87
kilometres around.

38
00:02:21,503 --> 00:02:28,979
Ten thousand superconducting magnets, each
one cooled with liquid helium to minus 269

39
00:02:28,979 --> 00:02:30,386
degrees Celsius.

40
00:02:30,706 --> 00:02:36,928
Two beams of protons, each carrying 20
trillion electron volts, colliding head on

41
00:02:36,928 --> 00:02:37,389
at 40.

42
00:02:37,709 --> 00:02:42,462
In 1988, the government picked a site in
Ellis County, Texas.

43
00:02:42,782 --> 00:02:48,291
The ground there was Austin Chalk. Soft
enough to bore through fast, solid enough

44
00:02:48,291 --> 00:02:50,263
to hold a tunnel for decades.

45
00:02:50,583 --> 00:02:54,910
By 1991, the giant boring machines were in
the ground.

46
00:02:55,230 --> 00:02:57,789
Seventeen shafts were sunk from the
surface.

47
00:02:58,109 --> 00:03:03,628
Laboratories went up. Two thousand people
moved to a small town called Waxahachie.

48
00:03:03,948 --> 00:03:08,895
And then, with the hardest part already
underway, Washington had something else to

49
00:03:08,895 --> 00:03:09,136
say.

50
00:03:13,256 --> 00:03:15,167
The first problem was the price.

51
00:03:15,487 --> 00:03:21,313
In 1987, the collider was pitched at about
4.4 billion dollars.

52
00:03:21,633 --> 00:03:27,943
By 1991, it had nearly doubled. By 1993,
it was over eleven billion.

53
00:03:28,263 --> 00:03:30,535
The second problem was the magnets.

54
00:03:30,855 --> 00:03:36,597
Early tests showed the beam might wander
inside its pipe. The fix was to widen the

55
00:03:36,597 --> 00:03:39,888
opening of every magnet by a single
centimetre.

56
00:03:40,208 --> 00:03:45,324
Multiplied across ten thousand magnets and
fifty-four miles of ring, that one

57
00:03:45,324 --> 00:03:47,052
centimetre cost a fortune.

58
00:03:47,372 --> 00:03:52,418
Then, the partners disappeared. The plan
assumed other countries would pay part of

59
00:03:52,418 --> 00:03:52,972
the bill.

60
00:03:53,292 --> 00:03:59,066
Japan was asked again and again. It never
committed. Almost nobody else did either.

61
00:03:59,386 --> 00:04:04,692
Then came the auditors. Congressional
investigators reported weak cost controls

62
00:04:04,692 --> 00:04:06,035
and poor management.

63
00:04:06,355 --> 00:04:08,343
Every report became a headline.

64
00:04:08,663 --> 00:04:13,291
But the biggest blow didn't come from
inside the project at all. It came from

65
00:04:13,291 --> 00:04:14,975
the other side of the world.

66
00:04:19,095 --> 00:04:22,453
In 1991, the Soviet Union collapsed.

67
00:04:22,773 --> 00:04:27,847
And with it went the argument that had
kept big American science funded for forty

68
00:04:27,847 --> 00:04:28,222
years.

69
00:04:28,542 --> 00:04:33,682
The collider had never been a weapon. But
it was born in a Cold War mindset: the

70
00:04:33,682 --> 00:04:37,729
country that understood the universe best
would lead the world.

71
00:04:38,049 --> 00:04:40,954
When the rival disappeared, so did the
urgency.

72
00:04:41,274 --> 00:04:46,007
Congress went looking for savings. And
there were two giant science projects on

73
00:04:46,007 --> 00:04:46,606
the table.

74
00:04:46,926 --> 00:04:48,935
The collider. And a space station.

75
00:04:49,255 --> 00:04:55,175
In June 1993, the space station survived a
vote in the House by a single vote.

76
00:04:55,495 --> 00:05:00,834
The collider wasn't so lucky. In October
1993, Congress cancelled it.

77
00:05:01,154 --> 00:05:06,770
The official reason was money. The deficit
was huge, and a machine only physicists

78
00:05:06,770 --> 00:05:09,442
could explain was an easy thing to cut.

79
00:05:09,762 --> 00:05:12,461
But the people who worked there have said
something else since.

80
00:05:12,781 --> 00:05:18,160
The project had lost its story. Nobody
outside physics could say why it mattered,

81
00:05:18,160 --> 00:05:21,083
and no amount of engineering could fix
that.

82
00:05:21,403 --> 00:05:25,465
For six years, Cold War ambition had kept
the collider alive.

83
00:05:25,785 --> 00:05:28,860
When the Cold War ended, the collider
ended with it.

84
00:05:29,180 --> 00:05:34,215
The physicists scattered. Many of them
ended up on Wall Street, building models

85
00:05:34,215 --> 00:05:34,852
for banks.

86
00:05:35,172 --> 00:05:40,914
In 2012, a smaller machine in Europe, the
Large Hadron Collider, found the Higgs

87
00:05:40,914 --> 00:05:41,344
boson.

88
00:05:41,664 --> 00:05:46,832
The discovery America had spent two
billion dollars chasing was announced in

89
00:05:46,832 --> 00:05:47,308
Geneva.

90
00:05:47,628 --> 00:05:51,585
That same year, the Texas site was sold to
a chemical company.

91
00:05:51,905 --> 00:05:56,501
Today, there's nothing to see. Just empty
buildings and a field of grass.

92
00:05:56,821 --> 00:06:01,446
Below it, fourteen miles of tunnel are
still down there, slowly filling with

93
00:06:01,446 --> 00:06:02,541
water in the dark.

94
00:06:02,861 --> 00:06:06,255
What would they have found at forty
trillion electron volts?

95
00:06:06,575 --> 00:06:07,751
Nobody knows.
